<div class="poster">

  <!-- HEADER -->
  <header class="header">
    <h1>The History of Books</h1>
    <h2>From Manuscripts to Mass Production</h2>
    <p class="subtitle">A DATA-DRIVEN LOOK AT 1,500 YEARS OF BOOK PRODUCTION &mdash; AND THE INVENTION THAT RESHAPED CIVILIZATION</p>
  </header>

  <!-- MAIN CHART: The Long Arc -->
  <section class="main-chart-section">
    <div class="section-header">
      <h3>The Long Arc of Book Production</h3>
      <span class="section-desc">Estimated annual new titles in Europe, 6th century to present (log scale)</span>
    </div>
    <div class="chart-container">
      <svg id="main-timeline" viewBox="0 0 2100 560" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"></svg>

      <!-- Gutenberg Annotation -->
      <div class="gutenberg-annotation">
        <div class="gut-year">~1440</div>
        <div class="gut-title">The Gutenberg Revolution</div>
        <div class="gut-desc">The printing press didn&rsquo;t just change publishing &mdash; it fundamentally reshaped civilization. Book output surged by orders of magnitude within decades.</div>
      </div>

      <!-- Manuscript Era Inset -->
      <div class="manuscript-inset">
        <h4>The Manuscript Era (6th&ndash;15th Century)</h4>
        <table>
          <tr><th>Century</th><th>Output</th><th>Driver</th></tr>
          <tr><td>6th</td><td>Very low</td><td>Minimal output</td></tr>
          <tr><td>9th</td><td>Moderate</td><td>Carolingian Renaissance</td></tr>
          <tr><td>12th</td><td>Notable</td><td>Rise of universities</td></tr>
          <tr><td>14th</td><td>Significant</td><td>Expanding elite literacy</td></tr>
          <tr><td>15th</td><td>Peak MS era</td><td>Eve of print</td></tr>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- KEY METRICS STRIP -->
  <section class="metrics-strip">
    <div class="metric-card">
      <div class="metric-value">~1440</div>
      <div class="metric-label">Gutenberg&rsquo;s Printing Press Invented</div>
    </div>
    <div class="metric-card">
      <div class="metric-value">10</div>
      <div class="metric-label">Centuries of Manuscript Data Covered (6th&ndash;15th C)</div>
    </div>
    <div class="metric-card">
      <div class="metric-value">1500&ndash;2009</div>
      <div class="metric-label">Printed Book Data Span (per half century &amp; per year)</div>
    </div>
    <div class="metric-card">
      <div class="metric-value">&gt;49</div>
      <div class="metric-label">Pages &mdash; UNESCO Definition of a Book</div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- MODERN PUBLISHING -->
  <section class="modern-section">
    <div class="modern-chart-wrap">
      <div id="country-chart" style="width:100%;height:100%;"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="modern-narrative">
      <h3>Modern Book Publishing</h3>
      <p>By the modern era, data tracks the number of <span class="highlight">new book titles published per million people</span> across countries, spanning from 1500 to 2009.</p>
      <p>Annual per capita book consumption varies widely by country, reflecting differences in <span class="highlight">education systems, income levels, and cultural reading habits</span>.</p>
      <p style="font-size:16px; color:var(--medium-brown); margin-top: 12px;">Source: Fink-Jensen (2015), IISH Data Collection</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- BOOKS AS MIRROR -->
  <section class="mirror-section">
    <div class="mirror-intro">
      <h3>Books as a Mirror of Society</h3>
      <p>Book production serves as a powerful proxy for several dimensions of human development. The transition from manuscripts to printed books &mdash; and from elite luxury to mass commodity &mdash; is one of the most important stories in the history of human knowledge.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="mirror-chart-wrap">
      <div id="mirror-chart" style="width:100%;height:100%;"></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- FOOTER -->
  <footer class="footer">
    <div class="sources">
      <strong>Sources:</strong> Buringh, E. &amp; Van Zanden, J.L. (2009). &ldquo;Charting the Rise of the West: Manuscripts and Printed Books in Europe.&rdquo; <em>The Journal of Economic History</em>, 69(2), 409&ndash;445. &bull; Fink-Jensen, J. (2015). IISH Data Collection. &bull; Roser, M. &amp; Ritchie, H. (2023). &ldquo;Books.&rdquo; <em>Our World in Data</em>. Licensed under CC BY.
    </div>
    <div class="branding">
      <div class="logo-text">Data &amp; Civilization</div>
      <div>A Visual History Series</div>
    </div>
  </footer>

</div>

